A `CancellationTokenSource` owns cancellation; the `CancellationToken` it hands out is a cheap struct you pass down every level of the call chain. Cancellation is **cooperative** — nothing is aborted, nothing is interrupted. A method that ignores the token runs to completion regardless, so an unpropagated token is the same as no cancellation at all.

The discipline: every async method takes a `CancellationToken` as its last parameter, passes it to every async call it makes, and calls `ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside any long loop or CPU-bound stretch that has no I/O to carry the token. The CA2016 analyzer flags the forwarding you forget.

In ASP.NET Core the token arrives for free: declare a `CancellationToken` parameter on a controller action or a minimal API handler and it is bound to `HttpContext.RequestAborted`, which fires when the client disconnects. Combining it with a timeout uses a linked source — and the linked source **must be disposed**, or it leaves a registration on the parent token that lives as long as the parent does:

```csharp
using var cts = CancellationTokenSource.CreateLinkedTokenSource(ct);
cts.CancelAfter(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
await _client.GetAsync(url, cts.Token);
```

Two judgement calls matter more than the mechanics. First, do not log `OperationCanceledException` as an error when it is your own token — a client hanging up is normal traffic, and treating it as a failure will drown your error rate. Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` in the `catch` to tell your cancellation from a downstream timeout. Second, **do not pass the request token to work that must complete.** Cancelling halfway through writing an audit record, committing an outbox row or finishing a multi-step save leaves inconsistent state. That work gets its own token, or none.

Java engineers: this is interruption or `Future.cancel`, equally cooperative. .NET has nothing like `Thread.Abort` any more — it throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on .NET Core.
